concepts.content:
When running an RNN for many timesteps, the computation graph can become very large. The gradient computation involves a series of matrix multiplications of length $\mathcal{O}(T)$. For example, computing the gradient $\frac{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}[5]}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[0]}$ involves the product:

$$\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}[t]}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[0]} = \frac{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}[t]}{\partial \mathbf{h}_T} \frac{\partial \mathbf{h}_T}{\partial \mathbf{h}_{T-1}} \cdots \frac{\partial \mathbf{h}_1}{\partial \mathbf{h}_0} \frac{\partial \mathbf{h}_0}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[0]}
\end{aligned}$$

Suppose the RNN uses ReLU nonlinearities. Then each term $\frac{\partial \mathbf{h}_t}{\partial \mathbf{h}_{t-1}}$ equals $\mathbf{R}\mathbf{W}$, where $\mathbf{R}$ is a diagonal matrix with zeros or ones depending on whether each neuron is active. This yields a product $\mathbf{R}_T\mathbf{W} \cdots \mathbf{R}_1\mathbf{W}$. In the worst case where all ReLUs are active, the gradient becomes $\mathbf{W}^T$ (with $T$ as an exponent). If values in $\mathbf{W}$ are large, the gradient explodes; if small, it vanishes. This numerical instability is problematic.

One solution is to use RNN units with better numerical properties, such as LSTMs, discussed in @sec-recurrent_neural_networks-LSTMS.

Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) units are a special kind of recurrent module designed to avoid the vanishing and exploding gradients problem described in @sec-recurrent_neural_networks-bptt (Hochreiter and Schmidhuber, 1997).

This presentation draws inspiration from a blog post by Chris Olah.

An LSTM acts like a memory controller with a cell state, $\mathbf{c}$, a vector it can read from and write to. The cell state records memories and retrieves them as needed. It plays a similar role as the hidden state, $\mathbf{h}$, from regular RNNs, but is built to store persistent memories that can last a long time (hence the name "long" short-term memory).

Like a normal recurrent layer, an LSTM takes as input a hidden state $\mathbf{h}_{t-1}$ and an observation $\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[t]$ and produces an updated hidden state $\mathbf{h}_t$. Internally, the LSTM uses its cell state to decide how to update $\mathbf{h}_{t-1}$.

First, the cell state is updated based on the incoming signals $\mathbf{h}_{t-1}$ and $\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[t]$. Then the cell state is used to determine the hidden state $\mathbf{h}_t$ to output. Different subcomponents of the LSTM decide what to delete from the cell state, what to write to it, and what to read off it.

One component picks the indices of the cell state to delete:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{f}_t &= \sigma(\mathbf{W}_f\mathbf{h}_{t-1} + \mathbf{U}_f\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[t] + \mathbf{b}_f) &\triangleleft \quad\quad\text{decide which indices to forget}
\end{aligned}$$

where $\mathbf{f}_t$ is a vector of length equal to the cell state $\mathbf{c}_t$, and $\sigma$ is the sigmoid function outputting values in $[0,1]$. Most values in $\mathbf{f}_t$ will be near either 1 (remember) or 0 (forget). Later, $\mathbf{f}_t$ is pointwise multiplied by the cell state to forget values where $\mathbf{f}_t$ is zero.

Another component chooses what values to write to the cell state and where:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{i}_t &= \sigma(\mathbf{W}_i\mathbf{h}_{t-1} + \mathbf{U}_i\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[t] + \mathbf{b}_i) &\triangleleft \quad\quad\text{which indices to write to}\\
    \tilde{\mathbf{c}}_t &= \texttt{tanh}(\mathbf{W}_C\mathbf{h}_{t-1} + \mathbf{U}_c\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[t] + \mathbf{b}_c) &\triangleleft \quad\quad\text{what to write to those indices}
\end{aligned}$$

where $\mathbf{i}_t$ is similar to $\mathbf{f}_t$ (a vector with values near 0 or 1) determining which indices of $\tilde{\mathbf{c}}_t$ will be written to the cell state. These vectors update the cell state:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{c}_t &= \mathbf{f}_t \odot \mathbf{c}_{t-1} + \mathbf{i}_t \odot \tilde{\mathbf{c}}_{t} &\triangleleft \quad\quad\text{update the cell state}
\end{aligned}$$

Finally, given the updated cell state, the LSTM reads from it to determine the hidden state output:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{o}_t &= \sigma(\mathbf{W}_o\mathbf{h}_{t-1} + \mathbf{U}_o\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[t] + \mathbf{b}_o) &\triangleleft \quad\quad\text{which indices of cell state to use}\\
    \mathbf{h}_t &= \mathbf{o}_t \odot \texttt{tanh}(\mathbf{c}_t) &\triangleleft \quad\quad\text{use these to determine next hidden state}
\end{aligned}$$

The basic idea is that by default it should be easy not to forget. The cell state controls what is remembered and can propagate forward through an identity connection from the previous to the subsequent cell state. This idea is similar to skip and residual connections in U-Nets and ResNets (@sec-convolutional_neural_nets).

Recurrent connections allow neural nets to maintain a persistent state representation that can be propagated forward or updated based on incoming experience. This feedback mechanism enables adaptive behavior and improved competence over time, analogous to closed-loop control in control theory.

